        <div class="reading-passage">
          <h3>How to Run a Great Interview</h3>
          <p>A great interview does not happen by accident — it requires careful preparation. Before the interview, skilled interviewers study the job description in detail and prepare a set of structured questions that directly relate to the role's competencies. This ensures that every candidate is assessed on the same criteria, making comparisons fair and meaningful. Walking into an interview without a plan wastes everyone's time and increases the risk of hiring the wrong person.</p>
          <p>During the interview, the most important skill is active listening. Interviewers should resist the temptation to talk about the company or the role for too long. A good rule of thumb is that the candidate should speak approximately 80% of the time. Rather than thinking about the next question while the candidate is speaking, a strong interviewer listens carefully and asks natural follow-up questions based on what has just been said. This signals genuine interest and often uncovers information that prepared questions alone would miss.</p>
          <p>One of the biggest challenges in interviewing is avoiding unconscious bias. Research consistently shows that interviewers are susceptible to being influenced by factors unrelated to the job — such as shared interests, the university a candidate attended, or even the sound of their name. To counteract this, many organisations require interviewers to score responses against pre-agreed competency criteria immediately after each answer, before the overall impression fades. Asking the same core questions to all candidates also helps create a level playing field.</p>
          <p>Finally, documentation matters. Interviewers should take brief notes during the conversation and write up a structured evaluation immediately after each interview. Memory is unreliable — especially when you are interviewing five or six candidates in a single day. Good documentation protects the organisation legally, supports fair decision-making, and makes it far easier to give constructive feedback to unsuccessful candidates who ask for it.</p>
