          <strong>1. Shared university:</strong> "Affinity bias" — feeling warmer towards someone similar to you. Counteract by scoring responses against objective competency criteria, not gut feeling.<br><br>
          <strong>2. Unfamiliar accent:</strong> Can trigger unconscious negative assumptions about capability. Counteract by separating communication clarity from language origin — ask yourself: did I understand the content of the answer?<br><br>
          <strong>3. CV gap for caregiving:</strong> Caregiving gaps are legitimate and common. Ask only job-relevant questions: "What skills or experiences did you gain during this period?"<br><br>
          <strong>4. Younger than expected:</strong> Age bias (positive or negative) is real. Focus on evidence of competence: "Can they demonstrate the experience the role requires?"<br><br>
          <strong>5. Casual dress:</strong> Dress codes vary across industries and cultures. Ask yourself: is this relevant to job performance? If the role has a dress code, address it factually in the offer stage — don't let it colour your assessment of their skills.
        </div>

